From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 16:36:57 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Embedding: hand segmentation to the host

An embedding host models x86 segmentation as Linux user code sees it,
the GDT the kernel gives every task, its TLS entries and LDT, and each
segment register's descriptor cache, for its other engine too, so
FEXCore leaves every instruction that reads or changes that state to
it: MOV and POP to a segment register, LDS, LES, LSS, LFS and LGS, far
CALL, JMP and RET, direct and indirect, IRET, and LAR, LSL, VERR, VERW,
SLDT and STR. Each stops the dispatch unretired with
EMBEDDER_SIGNAL_SEGMENT, and the host completes it, raises its fault,
or switches the execution mode. Outside 64-bit mode C4 and C5 with a
memory ModRM decode as LES and LDS, and 9A and EA, which the base
table never reached, as direct far CALL and JMP.

In compatibility mode an access through FS or GS first passes an
inline check of its offset against Embedder.SegmentChecks, a low bound
and a span per register that the host derives from the limit with room
for the widest access. A string instruction, XLAT, a gather, a masked
store, an extended-state image or a store through CS goes to the host
instead, unless Embedder.SegmentBypass is set. Either stops the
dispatch with EMBEDDER_SIGNAL_SEGMENT_CHECK, and the host checks the
access exactly and runs the instruction alone under the bypass. A
context made with SetEmbedderSegmented, for a task whose ES, SS or DS
has a base or a limit, adds each memory operand's segment base: SS for
an address based on ESP or EBP, and DS otherwise.

Selectors read from state in either mode, PUSH of a segment register
pushes its selector, and Embedder.SegmentBases and SegmentPresent go.
